00:00:09.589 --> 00:00:11.450 align:start position:0%
the u.s. system is designed to do  nothing all right so that means to get  something passed and to become a law you  need a majority in the house you need a  supermajority in the Senate you also  need the President to sign it and that's  aside from other institutional  constraints like the committee system  and so on all right so when the  Democrats hold the majority and the  Democrats hold the White House the u.s.  system is going to restrain their agenda  that's just the way the system is that's  the way the founders intended what we do  in this study is to try to ask the  question why why has the institution  evolved to look the way it does and so  what we do is we examine two  much-maligned aspects of the u.s.  political institution the supermajority  rule and the delegation of authority to  bureaucrats both of these are can be  seen as undemocratic the filibuster rule  in that it's not respecting majority  rule it's not representing the people as  well as it should and the delegation to  bureaucrats who are unelected all right  so it's often asked why he's already  delegated to these sorts of people who  aren't elected by the people  what we find is that these two  components the supermajority filibuster  rule and the delegation of authority to  unelected bureaucrats on their own seem  very undemocratic but together if they  work in such a way that their  interdependence actually leads to better  policy outcomes then we otherwise would  achieve what most analyses or most  commentary on the gridlock misses is  that there's a lot of action happening  in the background and what we're trying  to point out is a lot of this action is  happening via delegation so Congress may  fight they may be able to may not be  able to agree about whether the tax rate  should be a little higher or a little  lower but what they can agree upon in a  world that's changing over time is that  they want policy to be smoothed out in a  sense to not change too much as the  world changes to react to the changing  world because one thing that politicians  like particularly incumbent politicians  is they like the status quo they like  the world to not change because that  world is what got them elected and so as  much as they can they'd like to keep the  world the way it is gridlock serves that  purpose and so to that extent it's kind  of locking down the world or locking  down some policies for the interests of  these policymakers that allows deals to  be made in Congress and what we're  trying to show in this research is that  delegation is a way that Congress can  overcome the problems of gridlock and  get deals done  so to take a recent example we can look  at the dodd-frank bill where are lots of  authority was relegated to the financial  regulators now that may have been a  puzzle why didn't Congress they there's  nothing more sailing or nothing more  important in the last five years then  financial reform why didn't Congress  figure out the answer themselves what  they did was they delegate authority to  the regulators because they didn't know  how the world was going to change what  Congress people could agree upon is that  they wanted to find a solution and they  didn't know what it was and they didn't  know what it would be tomorrow but the  deal they struck was to delegate that  authority to a regulator who had  different preferences to them so it may  seem undemocratic but it's those  different preferences that allow the  deal to happen yesterday Democrats and  Republicans agreed something needed to  be done they could agree they didn't  know what it was but they could agree to  delegate authority to that agency  tomorrow when the agency starts figuring  out what the answer is Republicans or  maybe Democrats don't like it but they  can't backtrack they can't roll it back  precisely because of gridlock  gridlock has its pluses and has its  minuses all right it does stop things  from happening and it frustrates a lot  of people what we're trying to show here  is the gridlock may not be as bad as it  bad as it seems and that other aspects  of the political institution go a long  way to solving the problems or solving  the inefficiencies of gridlock
 

